---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import PlantExample from '../../components/PlantExample.astro';

// Four actors look at the same Alder Creek unit, each with their own question
const actors = [
  {
    actor: 'Operator',
    question: 'Are my turbines on schedule?',
    data: 'Hour counters, last inspection, manufacturer interval',
    finding: 'Gas turbine 2 has run 8% past its inspection interval, while the plan schedules it for 2027.',
  },
  {
    actor: 'Due diligence firm',
    question: 'Is the seller’s maintenance plan credible?',
    data: 'Planned schedule, completed inspections, actual durations and costs',
    finding: 'The 2027 inspection is planned at 12 days and 420 kUSD; the same one in 2025 took 15 days and cost 465 kUSD.',
  },
  {
    actor: 'Bank',
    question: 'What does maintenance cost the output that repays the debt?',
    data: 'Unit capacity, 2×1 configuration, production plan',
    finding: '306 MW lost during the outage: 2.4% of annual output according to the plan, 3% according to history.',
  },
  {
    actor: 'Trader',
    question: 'Which output have I sold that I will not be able to deliver, and when?',
    data: 'Outage calendar, lost capacity, forward sales, price curve over the period',
    finding: 'About 22,000 MWh to buy back if the inspection takes 15 days instead of 12.',
  },
];
---
<Page
  title="Ontology for Energy Assets"
  description="An ontology for energy assets: assets, findings, engagements and lenders, connected and reviewable."
  eyebrow="Solutions · Ontology for Energy Assets"
  heading="Assets, findings and lenders, connected."
  lead="ArborSpace ships with a first domain model for energy assets and due diligence engagements, so the firm starts from its own vocabulary, not from a blank page."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap doc-layout">
      <nav class="doc-toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <p class="eyebrow">Contents</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#captures">What the firm captures</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next-engagement">What changes on the next engagement</a></li>
          <li><a href="#actors">Actors</a></li>
          <li><a href="#example">An example: Alder Creek</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="captures">What the firm captures</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Thresholds and red flags</strong> your engineers apply, written as rules.</li>
          <li><strong>The links</strong> between each finding, the asset, the site, the engagement and the lender.</li>
          <li><strong>Every review decision</strong>, kept as part of the firm’s memory.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 id="next-engagement">What changes on the next engagement</h2>
        <p>
          A similarity engine matches each new case with the firm’s previous ones, and every
          conclusion traces back to a named rule that a lender’s reviewer can read.
        </p>

        <h2 id="actors">Actors: four views of the same project</h2>
        <p>
          The operator, the due diligence firm, the bank and the trader look at the same unit, but
          each asks their own question and needs different data to answer it. Brought together in
          one model, that data gives four answers in a few clicks.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Actor</th><th scope="col">Their question</th><th scope="col">The data they need</th><th scope="col">What they find at Alder Creek</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {actors.map((a) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{a.actor}</th><td>{a.question}</td><td>{a.data}</td><td>{a.finding}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <PlantExample />
</Page>
